{
  post: {
    title: 'Design Handover Problem',
    content: '<h1>Design handover problem</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<p>Learning from other project, we know that code generation is great for one way export, but fails on re-export. like any\n' +
      'development process, the work a designer does is never one off, but an iterative process at which the designer makes some design,\n' +
      'the developer builds some part of the application, then due to feedback (dev feedback, QA, product, early adopters, etc) the designer\n' +
      'updates the design, then the developer re-implements the design, etc.</p>\n' +
      '<p><img src="/content/design-log/jay/into-to-jay-1.png" alt="iterative design and feedback" loading="lazy" /> &#39;iterative design and feedback&#39;</p>\n' +
      '<p>Because there is no good tool for design export and re-export, we find that today, the way designers and developers are working\n' +
      'is by the <strong>designer designing and the developer <em>re-implementing</em> the design</strong> using code tools. The designer will export design\n' +
      'assets using tools like Adobe XP or photoshop, which are CSS snippets, image snippets, SVGs, etc. The developer, using those\n' +
      'design assets, will build the UI using tools like JSX, SSAS, etc.</p>\n' +
      '<p><img src="/content/design-log/jay/into-to-jay-2.png" alt="Comparing Jay with known workflow" loading="lazy" title="Comparing Jay with known workflow" /></p>\n' +
      '<p>Jay approach is to create a <strong>declarative format</strong> that can be generated from design tools, and used directly by the code.\n' +
      'This declarative format includes a contract that once coded against, the code does not change even if design changes drastically.\n' +
      'As the designer continues to update the design, the developer code who is using the design should inherit the update automatically.\n' +
      'Only if the contract between design and code changes, the developer will need to update the code - and should see type safe validations\n' +
      'that represent that contract change.</p>\n' +
      '<p>The next question is which format should the Jay file take? To answer that question we need to formulate the requirements from the Jay File -</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>should be declarative that a design tool can generate</li>\n' +
      '<li>should support any existing HTML / CSS capability</li>\n' +
      '<li>should support any future HTML / CSS capability</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<p>When examining this question and requirements, there is only one potential format that meets all of those - and that is HTML / CSS.</p>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Log Methodology Note</h2>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Note:</strong> These design logs are written primarily for AI agents as part of the <a href="/design-log">Design Log methodology</a> and made accessible here for human readers. The language and structure are optimized for machine consumption — expect precise, specification-style prose rather than narrative documentation.</p>\n',
    description: '',
    date: '',
    tags: [],
    frontmatter: '{"title":"Design Handover Problem","number":0}'
  }
}
[Build] 2/1235 /design-log/jay/[slug] (slug=000-design-handover-problem)
{
  post: {
    title: '3rd Party Code Problem',
    content: '<h1>Extending user interface with 3rd party components</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<p>The problem of extending user interfaces with 3rd party components is quite common - and has one known resolution.\n' +
      'Consider any kind of dashboard or website that needs to include components from a 3rd party while ensuring the 3rd party\n' +
      'does not have access to the host cookies, assets or REST APIs. The standard tool on the web is using <strong>IFrames</strong>, which\n' +
      'solves the problem using multiple domains, and the browser security model.</p>\n' +
      '<p>However, IFrames have a host of problems - from loading times to limited flexibility - no simple solution to overflow the IFrame boundaries\n' +
      'for things like modal dialogs. IFrames are not built for many small frames - consider a table at which each cell should be an IFrame...\n' +
      'or consider compositions of component in component...</p>\n' +
      '<p><img src="/content/design-log/jay/into-to-jay-3.png" alt="IFrame vs Jay security model" loading="lazy" title="IFrame vs Jay security model" /></p>\n' +
      '<p>Jay as a potential of solving this problem by introducing two new concepts - <strong>Jay Element</strong> and <strong>Jay Component</strong>.\n' +
      '<strong>Jay Element</strong> is what the Jay File contains, and what the developer imports. Because it is logic free, it is safe to\n' +
      'run on the main window. <strong>Jay Component</strong> is the code the developer writes that imports the Jay Element. It is unsafe as it includes\n' +
      '3rd party code - and those has to run inside an IFrame. However, Jay can run the <strong>all Jay Components in a single IFrame</strong> while supporting\n' +
      'multiple components on the page, including component in component and such.</p>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Log Methodology Note</h2>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Note:</strong> These design logs are written primarily for AI agents as part of the <a href="/design-log">Design Log methodology</a> and made accessible here for human readers. The language and structure are optimized for machine consumption — expect precise, specification-style prose rather than narrative documentation.</p>\n',
    description: '',
    date: '',
    tags: [],
    frontmatter: '{"title":"3rd Party Code Problem","number":1}'
  }
}
[Build] 3/1235 /design-log/jay/[slug] (slug=001-3rd-party-code-problem)
{
  post: {
    title: 'What Is Jay',
    content: '<h1>What is the Jay Project?</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<p>The Jay project started as an attempt to separate design from code and separate Design workflow\n' +
      'from Coding workflow, solving the handover problem Design -&gt; Code. It has evolved into solving\n' +
      'another problem of user interface inclusion from a 3rd party securely.</p>\n' +
      '<p>In a nutshell, the Jay Project is a Rendering Toolset that takes a Jay file, from which it compiles</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>a Runtime Component</li>\n' +
      '<li>a Typescript Definition</li>\n' +
      '<li>a Testing driver</li>\n' +
      '<li>&quot;run in secure context&quot; modules</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<div class="md-mermaid"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 960.9089999999999 201.8" width="960.9089999999999" height="201.8">\n' +
