[Build] 39/1235 /design-log/jay/[slug] (slug=123-deeply-nested-headfull-and-headless-components)
{
  post: {
    title: 'Contract Props And Params Consistency',
    content: '<h1>Design Log #124 — Contract Props and Params Consistency</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<h2>Background</h2>\n' +
      '<p>Contracts are the source of truth for the shape of a component. When a component accepts props (e.g., <code>productId</code>) or params (e.g., <code>slug</code>), these must be declared in the <code>.jay-contract</code> file. Without this, the agent-kit and validate tools cannot verify correctness, and AI agents generating pages may omit or mismatch props/params.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Related design logs: #38 (Contract File), #84 (headless props), #113 (explicit route params).</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Problem</h2>\n' +
      '<p>Components in consumer projects (e.g., the Wix mono repo) have contracts that are missing <code>props</code> and <code>params</code> declarations, even though the component implementation requires them. The agent-kit template already documents how to read and declare props/params in contracts, so the gap is in validation — nothing enforces consistency.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Concrete example: wix-stores-v1</h3>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token comment">// lib/components/product-page.ts</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">export</span> interface ProductPageParams <span class="token keyword">extends</span> UrlParams <span class="token punctuation">{</span> slug<span class="token operator">:</span> string<span class="token punctuation">;</span> <span class="token punctuation">}</span>\n' +
      '\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">export</span> <span class="token keyword">const</span> productPage <span class="token operator">=</span> makeJayStackComponent<span class="token operator">&lt;</span>ProductPageContract<span class="token operator">&gt;</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span>\n' +
      '    <span class="token punctuation">.</span>withProps<span class="token operator">&lt;</span>PageProps<span class="token operator">&gt;</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span>\n' +
      '    <span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token function">withLoadParams</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>loadProductParams<span class="token punctuation">)</span>  <span class="token comment">// yields ProductPageParams[]</span>\n' +
      '    <span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token punctuation">.</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-yaml"><span class="token comment"># product-page.jay-contract — NO params section!</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">name</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span> product<span class="token punctuation">-</span>page\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">tags</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token punctuation">-</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span> tag<span class="token punctuation">:</span> productName, type<span class="token punctuation">:</span> data, ... <span class="token punctuation">}</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>The component uses <code>.withLoadParams()</code> with <code>{ slug: string }</code>, but the contract has no <code>params</code>. The agent-kit doesn&#39;t know about <code>slug</code>, so AI-generated pages may not provide it.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Gap: Validate commands don&#39;t check component-contract consistency for props/params</h3>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>jay-stack validate</code> checks contract→route (does the route provide what contracts need), but not route→contract (are route params declared in some contract)</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>jay-stack validate-plugin</code> validates almost nothing about the component</li>\n' +
      '<li>Neither checks that <code>.withProps&lt;&gt;()</code> / <code>.withLoadParams&lt;&gt;()</code> usage matches contract declarations</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Files</strong>:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>packages/jay-stack/stack-cli/lib/validate.ts</code></li>\n' +
      '<li><code>packages/jay-stack/plugin-validator/lib/validate-plugin.ts</code></li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<h2>Questions</h2>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: What level of validation is feasible?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> Static checks at two levels: (a) jay-html attributes vs contract props, and (b) single-file AST analysis of component source to detect <code>.withProps&lt;&gt;()</code> / <code>.withLoadParams&lt;&gt;()</code> usage. The AST approach is proven by existing analyzers like <code>source-file-binding-resolver.ts</code>.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should validate-plugin check that the contract&#39;s props match the component&#39;s TypeScript signature?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> Yes — single-file AST check using <code>typescript-bridge</code>. This is the most important check (Phase 3). Detect builder method calls and extract type parameters to compare against contract.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Are there cases where a component has props but intentionally omits them from the contract?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> No. Props must be in the contract. The <code>props=&quot;{.}&quot;</code> pass-through pattern in client-only jay is not a prop declaration — the validator should skip <code>props</code> as an attribute name.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h2>Design</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>Phase 1: Route→contract param consistency (<code>checkRouteToContractParams</code>)</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>What:</strong> If a page is on a dynamic route, check that for each route param, at least one contract on the page (page-level or any keyed headless) declares it. One param might be consumed by the page contract, another by a headless component.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Rule:</strong> Collect all route params from the path. Collect all declared params from all contracts (page + headless). For each route param not in the combined set → warning.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Edge cases:</strong></p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>No contract on the page at all → skip (nothing to check against)</li>\n' +
      '<li>No contracts declare any params → warn for all route params</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<h3>Phase 2: Jay-html→contract prop consistency (<code>checkHeadlessInstanceProps</code>)</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>What:</strong> When a <code>&lt;jay:xxx&gt;</code> instance passes attributes, check that the resolved contract declares matching props. Also check that required contract props are present on the instance.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Skip attributes:</strong> <code>if</code>, <code>forEach</code>, <code>trackBy</code>, <code>ref</code>, <code>slowForEach</code>, <code>jayIndex</code>, <code>jayTrackBy</code>, <code>jay-coordinate-base</code>, <code>jay-scope</code>, <code>when-resolved</code>, <code>when-loading</code>, <code>when-rejected</code>, <code>accessor</code>, <code>props</code>, <code>key</code></p>\n' +
      '<h3>Phase 3: Component source→contract consistency (<code>checkComponentPropsAndParams</code>)</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>What:</strong> In validate-plugin, parse the component&#39;s TypeScript source and check:</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>If it calls <code>.withProps&lt;T&gt;()</code> with custom props → contract must declare <code>props</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>If it calls <code>.withLoadParams(...)</code> → contract must declare <code>params</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>Individual property names match between interface and contract</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<p><strong>AST patterns to detect:</strong></p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="">Builder chain:\n' +
      '  makeJayStackComponent&lt;ContractType&gt;()\n' +
      '    .withProps&lt;PropsType&gt;()           ← detect this\n' +
      '    .withLoadParams(loadFn)           ← detect this\n' +
      '    ...</code></pre>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Props type resolution:</strong></p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>.withProps&lt;PageProps&gt;()</code> → <code>PageProps</code> is the framework base type (<code>{ language, url }</code>). No custom props. Skip.</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>.withProps&lt;ProductCardProps&gt;()</code> → custom props. Find <code>interface ProductCardProps { productId: string }</code> in same file. Each property must be in contract <code>props</code>.</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>.withProps&lt;PageProps &amp; CustomProps&gt;()</code> → intersection. Strip <code>PageProps</code>, extract <code>CustomProps</code> properties.</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Params type resolution:</strong></p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>.withLoadParams(loadProductParams)</code> → find the function → look for the params interface it yields (e.g., <code>ProductPageParams extends UrlParams { slug: string }</code>)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Extract properties from the params interface (excluding inherited <code>UrlParams</code> fields)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Each property must be in contract <code>params</code></li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Framework types to skip:</strong></p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>PageProps</code> — framework base type, not component props</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>UrlParams</code> — base for params, provides inherited fields like <code>Record&lt;string, string&gt;</code></li>\n' +
      '<li><code>RequestQuery</code> — fast-phase only, not user-defined</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<h2>Implementation Plan</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>Phase 1: <code>checkRouteToContractParams</code></h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>File:</strong> <code>packages/jay-stack/stack-cli/lib/validate.ts</code></p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>Add <code>checkRouteToContractParams(parsedFile, filePath, pagesBase)</code>:<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Extract route params via <code>extractRouteParams</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>If no route params → return <code>[]</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>Collect all declared params from page contract + all headless import contracts</li>\n' +
      '<li>If no contracts exist at all → return <code>[]</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>For each route param not in the combined declared set → emit warning</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li>Call from <code>validateJayFiles</code> after existing <code>checkRouteParams</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>Add test fixtures + tests</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h3>Phase 2: <code>checkHeadlessInstanceProps</code></h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>File:</strong> <code>packages/jay-stack/stack-cli/lib/validate.ts</code></p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>Add <code>HEADLESS_SKIP_ATTRS</code> set (union of directive attrs + <code>props</code>, <code>key</code>)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Add <code>checkHeadlessInstanceProps(jayHtml, file)</code>:<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Walk body tree, find <code>&lt;jay:xxx&gt;</code> elements</li>\n' +
      '<li>Match to headless import by contr'... 9893 more characters,
    description: '',
    date: '',
    tags: [],
    frontmatter: '{"title":"Contract Props And Params Consistency","number":124}'
  }
}
[Build] 40/1235 /design-log/jay/[slug] (slug=124-contract-props-and-params-consistency)
{
  post: {
    title: 'Plugin Agent-Kit',
    content: '<h1>Design Log #125 — Plugin Agent-Kit</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<h2>Background</h2>\n' +
      '<p>The existing <code>jay-stack agent-kit</code> command generates instructions and reference data for AI agents that build <strong>jay-stack projects</strong> — creating jay-html pages that consume plugins. But creating a <strong>plugin</strong> is a fundamentally different task: defining contracts, writing headless components, setting up <code>plugin.yaml</code>, implementing server actions, etc.</p>\n' +
      '<p>There is no agent-kit support for this workflow today. AI agents creating plugins have no structured guidance, which leads to issues like contracts missing <code>props</code> and <code>params</code> declarations (see DL#124).</p>\n' +
      '<p>Related design logs: #39 (Plugin package), #60 (plugin system refinement), #84 (headless props), #85 (agent-kit for projects), #124 (contract consistency).</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Problem</h2>\n' +
      '<p>An AI agent tasked with creating or modifying a jay plugin has no equivalent of the project agent-kit. It must rely on general knowledge or <code>.cursor/skills/</code> files (which are local to the jay framework repo, not installed into consumer projects). This leads to:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Contracts that are incomplete (missing props, params, phases)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Components that don&#39;t match their contracts</li>\n' +
      '<li>Missing or malformed <code>plugin.yaml</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>Actions without <code>.jay-action</code> metadata files</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<h2>Questions</h2>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should <code>--mode plugin</code> generate into the same <code>agent-kit/</code> folder or a different one (e.g., <code>agent-kit-plugin/</code>)?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> <del>Separate <code>agent-kit-plugin/</code> folder.</del> Revised: all three agent-kits (designer, developer, plugin) live under one <code>agent-kit/</code> folder as subfolders. See point 3 below.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: What reference data should the plugin agent-kit generate?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Documentation/skills focused on plugin authoring:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Component state hooks</strong>: <code>createSignal</code>, <code>createMemo</code>, <code>createEffect</code>, <code>createEvent</code> for the interactive phase</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Refs</strong>: how to use refs, collection refs</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Immutable data</strong>: data is immutable, examples of using JSON Patch to update data, the <code>map</code> hook</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Contract data types</strong>: all tag types (data, variant, interactive, sub-contract), async data types (<code>Promise&lt;T&gt;</code>), linked sub-contracts, repeated sub-contracts with <code>trackBy</code></li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Interactive elements</strong>: interactive refs, element types</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Phase guidance</strong>: how to decide if data is <code>slow</code>, <code>fast</code>, or <code>fast+interactive</code> — what belongs at build time vs request time vs client-side</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Props vs params</strong>: when to use <code>props</code> (component configuration, passed by parent) vs <code>params</code> (URL route segments, passed by routing)</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should the plugin agent-kit include validation instructions (run <code>jay-stack validate-plugin</code>)?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Yes.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Is a plugin always developed inside a standalone package, or can it be developed inline within a project?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> It can be developed inline within a project. See <code>examples/jay-stack/fake-shop</code> for an example.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: How does a plugin describe which contract/action to use when?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Add a <code>description</code> field to <code>.jay-contract</code> files (<code>.jay-action</code> already has one). The <code>agent-kit</code> command extracts descriptions into the materialized index, giving agents a quick overview without opening every file. <code>plugin.yaml</code> keeps its short <code>description</code> per contract as a summary for indexing.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: How does a plugin tell the agent about its reference files?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Two parts: (1) plugin declares its references in <code>plugin.yaml</code> so the system knows they exist, (2) the <code>agent-kit</code> command generates an <code>INDEX.md</code> per plugin in <code>references/&lt;plugin&gt;/</code> describing what each reference file contains and how to use it.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should there be three agent-kits instead of two?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Yes. Three roles: <strong>designer</strong> (creates jay-html UI), <strong>developer</strong> (builds the project, wiring, config), <strong>plugin</strong> (creates contracts, components, actions). All under one <code>agent-kit/</code> folder as subfolders.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should <code>agent-kit</code> work before <code>setup</code>?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Yes. Before setup: generates instruction/guide files only, notes which plugins are not initialized (as info, not errors). After setup: also materializes contracts and references.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should plugins be able to contribute skills to each agent-kit?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Yes. A plugin package can include an <code>agent-kit/</code> folder with subfolders matching the three roles (<code>designer/</code>, <code>developer/</code>, <code>plugin/</code>). The <code>agent-kit</code> command merges these into the project&#39;s agent-kit.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: What should we call the three folders and their instruction files?</strong>\n' +
      '<strong>A:</strong> Folders: <code>designer/</code>, <code>developer/</code>, <code>plugin/</code> — these are roles, not agents. Instruction files: <strong>guides</strong> (not &quot;skills&quot; to avoid confusion with <code>.cursor/skills/</code>). Each folder has an <code>INSTRUCTIONS.md</code> as the index pointing to the guides.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h2>Design</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>CLI Interface</h3>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-bash"><span class="token function">jay-stack</span> <span class="token function">agent-kit</span>              <span class="token comment"># generates all three roles</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token function">jay-stack</span> <span class="token function">agent-kit</span> --<span class="token function">mode</span> <span class="token function">plugin</span>    <span class="token comment"># generate only plugin guides</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token function">jay-stack</span> <span class="token function">agent-kit</span> --<span class="token function">mode</span> <span class="token function">designer</span>  <span class="token comment"># generate only designer guides</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token function">jay-stack</span> <span class="token function">agent-kit</span> --<span class="token function">mode</span> <span class="token function">developer</span> <span class="token comment"># generate only developer guides</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>Before <code>jay-stack setup</code>: generates guide files only, notes uninitialized plugins as info (not errors).\n' +
      'After <code>jay-stack setup</code>: also materializes contracts and references.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Document Size Guideline</h3>\n' +
      '<p>Each guide file should aim for <strong>100–200 lines</strong>. If a document exceeds ~250 lines, split it into focused sub-documents. The structure below is the initial split — it is flexible and should be adjusted based on actual content length during implementation.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Unified Agent-Kit Output</h3>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="">agent-kit/\n' +
      '├── references/                    # Plugin-generated reference data\n' +
      '│   └── &lt;plugin-name&gt;/\n' +
      '│       ├── INDEX.md               # Describes each reference file\n' +
      '│       └── ...reference files\n' +
      '├── materialized-contracts/        # Materialized contract files\n' +
      '│\n' +
      '├── designer/                      # Role: creates jay-html UI\n' +
      '│   ├── INSTRUCTIONS.md            # Workflow + index of guides\n' +
      '│   ├── jay-html-syntax.md\n' +
      '│   ├── jay-html-template-syntax.md\n' +
      '│   ├── jay-html-components.md\n' +
      '│   ├── jay-html-styling.md\n' +
      '│   ├── routing.md\n' +
      '│   ├── contracts-and-plugins.md   # Reading contracts, mapping to jay-html\n' +
      '│   ├── cli-commands.md\n' +
      '│   └── &lt;plugin-contributed&gt;*.md   # Plugins can add designer guides\n' +
      '│\n' +
      '├── developer/                     # Role: builds the project + page components\n' +
      '│   ├── INSTRUCTIONS.md\n' +
      '│   ├── project-structure.md\n' +
      '│   ├── routing.md\n' +
      '│   ├── configuration.md           # .jay, plugin config, init\n' +
      '│   ├── page-contracts.md          # Page-level contracts (page.jay-contract)\n' +
      '│   ├── page-components.md         # page.ts: makeJayStackComponent for pages\n' +
      '│   ├── component-state.md         # createSignal, createMemo, createEffect, etc.\n' +
      '│   ├── component-refs.md          # Refs, collection refs\n' +
      '│   ├── component-data.md          # Immutable data, JSON Patch, map\n' +
      '│   ├── render-results.md          # phaseOutput, RenderPipeline, errors, redirects\n' +
      '│   ├── cli-commands.md\n' +
      '│   └── &lt;plugin-contributed&gt;*.md   # Plugins can add developer guides\n' +
      '│\n' +
      '└── plugin/                        # Role: creates plugins (contracts, components, actions)\n' +
      '    ├── INSTRUCTIONS.md\n' +
      '    ├── contracts-guide.md\n' +
      '    ├── plugin-structure.md\n' +
      '    ├── component-structure.md\n' +
      '    ├── component-state.md\n' +
