{
  post: {
    title: 'Streaming Actions',
    content: '<h1>Design Log #129 — Streaming Actions (Generator Support)</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<h2>Background</h2>\n' +
      '<p>Jay actions (<code>makeJayAction</code> / <code>makeJayQuery</code>) currently support a request-response model: the client sends input, the server handler returns a single <code>Promise&lt;Output&gt;</code>, and the client receives the result.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Some use cases require multiple responses from a single request — streaming results as they become available. Examples:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><strong><code>loadRouteParams</code></strong>: a generator that yields batches of URL params as they&#39;re discovered from an API (e.g., paginated product listings)</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>AI agent chat</strong>: streaming tokens from an LLM response</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Search with progressive refinement</strong>: results arriving as different data sources respond</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Long-running operations</strong>: progress updates during build/deploy</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<p>Related design logs: #63 (server actions), #82 (auto service injection), #128 (page freeze — <code>loadRouteParams</code> via editor protocol).</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Problem</h2>\n' +
      '<p>The current action system is strictly request → response:</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token comment">// Current: single response</span>\n' +
      `<span class="token keyword">export</span> <span class="token keyword">const</span> searchProducts <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token function">makeJayAction</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'products.search'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token function">withHandler</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token function">async </span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>input<span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">=&gt;</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span>\n` +
      '  <span class="token keyword">const</span> results <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token keyword">await</span> db<span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token function">search</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>input<span class="token punctuation">.</span>query<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token keyword">return</span> results<span class="token punctuation">;</span> <span class="token comment">// Single response</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token punctuation">}</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>For <code>loadRouteParams</code>, we worked around this by adding a custom editor protocol message with socket events. But this pattern doesn&#39;t generalize — each streaming use case would need its own protocol extension.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Current <code>loadRouteParams</code> workaround</h3>\n' +
      `<pre class="md-code"><code class="">Client → { type: 'loadRouteParams', route: '/products/[slug]' }\n` +
      "Server → { type: 'loadRouteParams', success: true }  // Ack\n" +
      "Server → emit('routeParamsBatch', { params: [...], hasMore: true })  // Event\n" +
      "Server → emit('routeParamsBatch', { params: [...], hasMore: true })  // Event\n" +
      "Server → emit('routeParamsBatch', { params: [], hasMore: false })    // Done</code></pre>\n" +
      '<p>This works but requires protocol-level extensions for each streaming case.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Questions</h2>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should streaming actions use HTTP (SSE/chunked transfer) or WebSocket?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> Plain HTTP with chunked transfer encoding. A single HTTP request, single response — but the response body is written incrementally as the generator yields. The client reads chunks one by one. This is simpler than SSE/WebSocket, works through proxies and CDNs, and can be cached. It doesn&#39;t support generic server→client push, but it does support the streaming-data-from-server pattern, which is what generator actions need.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should the handler return <code>AsyncGenerator&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>AsyncIterable&lt;T&gt;</code>?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> <code>AsyncGenerator&lt;T&gt;</code> — the natural fit for a generator function (<code>async function*</code>). <code>AsyncIterable&lt;T&gt;</code> is the consumed interface; the handler produces via generator.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: How does the client consume streamed responses?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> As a generator as well — the client-side action returns <code>AsyncGenerator&lt;Chunk&gt;</code>, consumed via <code>for await...of</code>. The client reads the chunked HTTP response, parses each chunk boundary (newline-delimited JSON), and yields each parsed chunk.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: Should streaming be a new action type or an extension of <code>makeJayAction</code>?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> New builder — <code>makeJayStream</code> (or <code>makeJayGeneratorAction</code>). Streaming has different semantics (generator vs promise, chunked vs single response) and keeping it separate makes the API clear.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Q: How does this interact with the <code>.jay-action</code> metadata schema?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>A:</strong> Same <code>.jay-action</code> file format, with a <code>streaming: true</code> field to indicate the action yields multiple chunks. The <code>outputSchema</code> describes the shape of each chunk (not the full response).</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h2>Design</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>Handler: <code>AsyncGenerator</code> return type</h3>\n' +
      `<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token keyword">export</span> <span class="token keyword">const</span> discoverParams <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token function">makeJayStream</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'routes.discoverParams'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span>\n` +
      '  <span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token function">withServices</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>PRODUCTS_SERVICE<span class="token punctuation">)</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token function">withHandler</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token keyword">async</span> <span class="token keyword">function</span><span class="token operator">*</span> <span class="token punctuation">(</span>input<span class="token punctuation">,</span> productsService<span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span>\n' +
      '    <span class="token keyword">let</span> page <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token number">1</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '    <span class="token function">while </span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token keyword">true</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span>\n' +
