[Build] 62/1235 /design-log/jay/[slug] (slug=140-production-smoke-test)
{
  post: {
    title: 'Fast Phase Request Context',
    content: '<h1>DL#141: Fast-Phase Request Context (Cookies &amp; Response Headers)</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<h2>Background</h2>\n' +
      '<p>The wix-members package needs two framework capabilities that don&#39;t exist yet: reading HTTP cookies during the fast phase, and setting HTTP response headers (specifically <code>Cache-Control</code>) from the fast phase. A third requirement — returning redirects/errors from the fast phase — is already fully supported via <code>redirect3xx()</code>, <code>forbidden()</code>, etc. (DL#54).</p>\n' +
      '<p>The fast phase already receives per-request data: <code>PageProps</code> (language, url), path params, and <code>RequestQuery</code> (DL#117). But the HTTP request is not exposed beyond these. And <code>PhaseOutput</code> carries data back (ViewState, carryForward, headTags) but has no mechanism for response headers.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Use cases</h3>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Login-protected page</strong>: The fast phase reads a member auth token from a cookie. If invalid/missing, it returns <code>redirect3xx(302, &#39;/login&#39;)</code>. If valid, it renders member-specific content.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Cache-Control on member pages</strong>: When a page renders with member-specific data, the component&#39;s fast phase sets <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> so the response isn&#39;t cached by CDN or browser.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Login indicator on regular pages</strong>: Purely client-side (interactive phase reads cookies via <code>document.cookie</code>). Regular pages stay cacheable. No framework changes needed for this case.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h2>Problem</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>1. No cookie access in fast phase</h3>\n' +
      '<p><code>PageProps</code> contains only <code>language</code> and <code>url</code>. The HTTP request object (<code>req</code>) exists in dev-server and production-server handlers but isn&#39;t forwarded to the fast phase. Components wanting to read cookies have no path to do so.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Current flow:</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="">HTTP Request (has cookies in headers)\n' +
      '    ↓\n' +
      'dev-server.ts / main-server.ts (has req object)\n' +
      '    ↓\n' +
      'renderFastChangingData(pageParams, pageProps, ..., query)  ← no cookies\n' +
      '    ↓\n' +
      'compDefinition.fastRender(props, ...)  ← props has no cookies</code></pre>\n' +
      '<h3>2. No response headers from fast phase</h3>\n' +
      '<p><code>PhaseOutput</code> returns <code>{ kind, rendered, carryForward, headTags? }</code>. There&#39;s no field for response headers. The response status/headers are set by the server layer after the fast phase completes, but only with hardcoded values (<code>200</code>, <code>Content-Type: text/html</code>).</p>\n' +
      '<p>Components can influence <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> content via <code>headTags</code> (DL#127), but <code>headTags</code> are HTML elements injected into the page body — they&#39;re not HTTP headers.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Questions and Answers</h2>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Q1: Should cookies be a separate prop (like <code>query</code>) or part of a broader request context?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p>A1: Separate prop. Following the DL#117 pattern, <code>cookies</code> is a <code>Record&lt;string, string&gt;</code> intersected into the fast phase props type. This keeps the API consistent with <code>query</code> — both are per-request data available only in the fast phase. Adding a broad <code>request</code> object would leak HTTP concerns into the component model.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Q2: Should cookies be available in the slow phase?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p>A2: No. Same reasoning as query params (DL#117) — slow phase is cached/SSG, cookies are per-request. Including cookies would bust the cache or serve stale content.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Q3: Should response headers be on <code>PhaseOutput</code> (like <code>headTags</code>) or a separate mechanism?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p>A3: On <code>PhaseOutput</code>, in the existing <code>options</code> bag. This follows the <code>headTags</code> pattern exactly — optional metadata that flows through the render pipeline and gets applied by the server layer. The <code>phaseOutput()</code> helper already accepts <code>options?: { headTags?: HeadTag[] }</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Q4: Should we support setting cookies (Set-Cookie response header) from the fast phase?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p>A4: Not in this design. <code>Set-Cookie</code> has complex semantics (domain, path, secure, httpOnly, sameSite, maxAge). The wix-members use case only needs to <em>read</em> cookies, not write them. Setting cookies can be added later via a typed <code>responseCookies</code> field if needed.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Q5: How does this interact with the DL#139 fetch handler?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p>A5: DL#139 is implemented. The production server now uses Fetch API handlers: <code>fetchPageRequest()</code> in <code>fetch-page-handler.ts</code> returns <code>Response</code> with <code>ReadableStream</code>, and <code>createJayFetchHandler()</code> in <code>@jay-framework/jay-fetch-handler</code> receives the full <code>Request</code> object. Cookies are parsed from <code>request.headers.get(&#39;cookie&#39;)</code> at the handler level and passed down to <code>fetchPageRequest()</code> → <code>renderFastChangingData()</code>. Response headers from <code>PhaseOutput</code> are spread into <code>new Response(stream, { headers: { ...responseHeaders } })</code>. The old Node.js handlers (<code>page-handler.ts</code>) are kept but no longer used by <code>main-server.ts</code> — they don&#39;t need updating.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Q6: Should response headers merge across multiple page parts (like headTags do)?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p>A6: Last-write-wins, same ordering as headTags: headless components first (ordered by template position), page component last. For <code>Cache-Control</code> specifically, the most restrictive value should win — if any component says <code>no-store</code>, the page is <code>no-store</code>. But implementing &quot;most restrictive&quot; requires Cache-Control-specific logic. Simpler: last-write-wins. The page component runs last and can always override. If a plugin needs <code>no-store</code>, the plugin sets it and the page component shouldn&#39;t override it. In practice: collisions are unlikely for response headers, and when they occur the page author has final say.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Q7: What about the <code>handleOtherResponseCodes</code> path — does it need response headers?</strong></p>\n' +
      '<p>A7: No. Redirects and errors are short-circuit responses with their own status codes and headers. Response headers from <code>PhaseOutput</code> only apply to successful (200) responses. A <code>redirect3xx</code> already sets <code>Location</code> header. A <code>forbidden</code> sets status 403. Adding <code>Cache-Control</code> to error responses is not a use case.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Design</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>1. Request cookies in fast phase</h3>\n' +
      '<p>New type <code>RequestCookies</code>, intersected into <code>RenderFast</code> props alongside <code>RequestQuery</code>:</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token comment">// jay-stack-types.ts</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">export</span> interface RequestCookies <span class="token punctuation">{</span>\n' +
      '  cookies<span class="token operator">:</span> Record<span class="token operator">&lt;</span>string<span class="token punctuation">,</span> string<span class="token operator">&gt;</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token punctuation">}</span>\n' +
      '\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">export</span> type RenderFast<span class="token operator">&lt;</span>Services<span class="token punctuation">,</span> PropsT<span class="token punctuation">,</span> FastViewState<span class="token punctuation">,</span> FastCarryForward<span class="token operator">&gt;</span> <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token punctuation">(</span>\n' +
      '  props<span class="token operator">:</span> PropsT <span class="token operator">&amp;</span> RequestQuery <span class="token operator">&amp;</span> RequestCookies<span class="token punctuation">,</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token punctuation">.</span>services<span class="token operator">:</span> Services\n' +
      '<span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">=&gt;</span> Promise<span class="token operator">&lt;</span>FastRenderResult<span class="token operator">&lt;</span>FastViewState<span class="token punctuation">,</span> FastCarryForward<span class="token operator">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>Usage in a component:</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token function">withFastRender</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token function">async </span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>props<span class="token punctuation">,</span> carryForward<span class="token punctuation">,</span> memberService<span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">=&gt;</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span>\n' +
      `    <span class="token keyword">const</span> token <span class="token operator">=</span> props<span class="token punctuation">.</span>cookies<span class="token punctuation">[</span><span class="token string">'session-token'</span><span class="token punctuation">]</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n` +
      `    <span class="token function">if </span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token operator">!</span>token<span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token keyword">return</span> <span class="token function">redirect3xx</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">302</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token string">'/login'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n` +
      '\n' +
      '    <span class="token keyword">const</span> member <span class="token operato'... 39948 more characters,
