{
  post: {
    title: 'Main To Worker Communication Protocol',
    content: '<h1>Main to Worker Communication Protocol</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<p>This document tries to capture the challenges in defining and creating the communication\n' +
      'protocol between the main window (running elements and component bridges) and the\n' +
      'sandbox (running components and element bridges).</p>\n' +
      '<h2>The protocol</h2>\n' +
      '<p>On secure context initial creation:</p>\n' +
      '<p><img src="/content/design-log/jay/15-main-to-worker-communication-protocol-1-md.png" alt="Jay Communication Protocol 1" loading="lazy" /></p>\n' +
      '<p>The main window sends a <code>JPMRootComponentProps</code> message with the <code>ViewState</code> of the element\n' +
      'opting in to use a secure component. The worker root is using this <code>ViewState</code> to start\n' +
      'the rendering process in the sandbox environment.</p>\n' +
      '<p>As the rending of components continues within the sandbox environment, each element bridge\n' +
      'records the input <code>ViewState</code> it gets and sends a <code>JPMRender</code> message to it&#39;s corresponding\n' +
      'component bridge.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Two important things to remember here -</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>for performance, we do not compute component props on the main window, rather, we compute\n' +
      'props in the sandbox. We render all the tree in the sandbox, recording all the <code>JPMRender</code> messages.</li>\n' +
      '<li>again for performance, while the protocol models multiple <code>JPMRender</code> messages, the underlying\n' +
      'channel only sends a single low level message that batches all the protocol messages.</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<p><img src="/content/design-log/jay/15-main-to-worker-communication-protocol-2-md.png" alt="Jay Communication Protocol 2" loading="lazy" /></p>\n' +
      '<p>When registering events, the element bridge sends a <code>JPMAddEventListener</code> message to the\n' +
      'component bridge, with the <code>refName</code> of the sub-element to add the event for, and <code>eventType</code>\n' +
      'to listen for.</p>\n' +
      '<p>When an event is triggered, the component bridge captures the event. For regular events,\n' +
      'it only sends the <code>coordinate</code> and <code>eventType</code> of the actual event. For events registered\n' +
      'using <code>$onX</code>, it also adds the computed <code>eventData</code>. The data is sent as a <code>JPMDomEvent</code>\n' +
      'message to the element bridge, who triggers the event on the original component</p>\n' +
      '<h2>The addressing challenge</h2>\n' +
      '<p>As we can see above, one of the challenges is directing messages between the pair of component and\n' +
      'element bridges at the same place in the tree. Because the tree structure, <code>forEach</code> and <code>if</code>\n' +
      'statements, the tree structure cannot be statically determined.</p>\n' +
      '<p>We denote <code>CompId</code> as a <code>number</code> value that facilitates the communication between the bridges - that is,\n' +
      'component bridge <code>CompId: 2</code> communicates with element bridge <code>CompId: 2</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p><img src="/content/design-log/jay/15-main-to-worker-communication-protocol-3-md.png" alt="Jay Communication Protocol 3" loading="lazy" /></p>\n' +
      '<p>The derive the relationships between element bridge and component bridge, we need to look\n' +
      'at the parent component and the coordinate within the parent component&#39;s element.\n' +
      'That is, we compute <code>CompId</code> based on <code>(parent CompId, coordinate in parent)</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p><code>CompId:2</code> ~~ <code>CompId:1</code> + <code>Coordinate: a</code></p>\n' +
      '<p>We also need to think of the order of components and elements creation - the main point of\n' +
      'which is that a child is always created after the parent, and has the creation context\n' +
      'internally in the runtime package. It terms of <code>CompId</code> allocation - who allocates between the\n' +
      'main and sandbox, there is no one clear answer - for static elements and components, the\n' +
      'main window will be first. For dynamic elements and components, the sandbox will be first.</p>\n' +
      '<p>For the element bridge <code>CompId: 2</code> to generate the <code>CompId</code>, it needs to look up the stack\n' +
      'for the element bridge <code>CompId: 1</code> and to the <code>coordinate: a</code> of a sub-element of <code>CompId: 1</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p>For the component bridge <code>CompId: 2</code> to generate the <code>CompId</code>, it needs to look up the stack\n' +
      'for the component bridge <code>CompId: 1</code> and to the <code>coordinate: a</code> of a sub-element of <code>CompId: 1</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Both imply a context API for Jay that can be used by the secure package to access the parent\n' +
      '<code>CompId</code> and <code>coordinate</code>. See <a href="16-context-api">16 - Context API</a></p>\n' +
      '<h2>The formal communication protocol</h2>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token keyword">export</span> interface JayPort <span class="token punctuation">{</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token function">getRootEndpoint</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token operator">:</span> JayEndpoint<span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token function">getEndpoint</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>parentCompId<span class="token operator">:</span> number<span class="token punctuation">,</span> parentCoordinate<span class="token operator">:</span> string<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token operator">:</span> JayEndpoint<span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token function">batch</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>handler<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">=&gt;</span> <span class="token keyword">void</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token function">flush</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token punctuation">}</span>\n' +
      '\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">export</span> interface JayEndpoint <span class="token punctuation">{</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token function">post</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>outMessage<span class="token operator">:</span> JayPortMessage<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token function">onUpdate</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>handler<span class="token operator">:</span> JayPortInMessageHandler<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '  get <span class="token function">compId</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token operator">:</span> number<span class="token punctuation">;</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token punctuation">}</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>JayPort</code> - the connection to the protocol. Each side, the main and the sandbox, have\n' +
      'one port to facilitate the communication<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>getRootEndpoint</code> - gets an endpoint that represents the tree root, that is, does not\n' +
      'have a parent component</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>getEndpoint</code> - gets an endpoint that represents a specific bridge communication with it&#39;s partner bridge.\n' +
      'the call to <code>getEndpoint</code> also triggers the generation of the <code>CompId</code> based of the provided parameters</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>batch</code> - record all messages send via the endpoints during the handler run,\n' +
      'and flush all messages as one batch when the handler completes</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>flush</code> - flushes all messages, if messages are added without <code>batch</code></li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>JayEndpoint</code> - the actual interface bridges are using<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>post</code> - send a message to the partner bridge</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>onUpdate</code> - listen to messages from the partner bridge</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>compId</code> - get the compId.</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Log Methodology Note</h2>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Note:</strong> These design logs are written primarily for AI agents as part of the <a href="/design-log">Design Log methodology</a> and made accessible here for human readers. The language and structure are optimized for machine consumption — expect precise, specification-style prose rather than narrative documentation.</p>\n',
    description: '',
    date: '',
    tags: [],
    frontmatter: '{"title":"Main To Worker Communication Protocol","number":15}'
  }
}
