{
  post: {
    title: '`Jay Start` - Compiling Sandbox Application',
    content: '<h1>Compiling Sandboxed Application</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<p>The challenge we face is to take a single application folder, which consists of (now) of Jay components <code>.ts</code> files\n' +
      'and Jay element <code>.jay.html</code> files, and build out of it two scripts - one for the main window, and a second one for\n' +
      'a sandbox environment, like a worker.</p>\n' +
      '<p>The challenge involves a few moving parts -</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>how to identify from which component to start the sandbox?</li>\n' +
      '<li>once starting to sandbox components, the main script has to generate component bridges - how do we track that point in the bundling tree?</li>\n' +
      '<li>once starting to sandbox components, the sandbox script has to generate element bridges - how to start the sandbox script indicating the entry point components?</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h2>problem 1 - identifying the components to sandbox</h2>\n' +
      '<p>The approach we are taking is to mark sandbox as part of a jay element file imports, such as</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-html"><span class="token tag">&lt;link</span> <span class="token attribute">rel</span><span class="token punctuation">=</span><span class="token string">&quot;import&quot;</span> <span class="token attribute">href</span><span class="token punctuation">=</span><span class="token string">&quot;./counter&quot;</span> <span class="token attribute">names</span><span class="token punctuation">=</span><span class="token string">&quot;Counter&quot;</span> <span class="token attribute">sandbox</span><span class="token punctuation">=</span><span class="token string">&quot;yes&quot;</span> <span class="token punctuation">/</span><span class="token punctuation">&gt;</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>where the <code>sandbox</code> attribute can give a semantic name for the sandbox, allowing to run multiple components in the same sandbox.\n' +
      'for now, we focus on creating the initial sandbox (one sandbox), but later we can expand the notion to multiple sandboxes.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Once identified a jay element file with a <code>sandbox</code> indicator, we generate the element with support for sandboxed child components.\n' +
      'This generation happens with the <a href="../packages/compiler/lib/jay-file/jay-file-compiler.ts#883"><code>generateElementFile</code></a>\n' +
      'function, and should return, aside from the generated file, also the\n' +
      'list of sandboxed child components.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>problem 2 - bundling component bridges in main script</h2>\n' +
      '<p>From this point - the element that indicates inporting sandboxed components, we need to track the import linage as\n' +
      'we create the bundle. Each component that it&#39;s linage has one of the sandboxed components as to be bundled as a component bridge\n' +
      'created using the <a href="../packages/compiler/lib/ts-file/transform-component-bridge.ts"><code>componentBridgeTransformer</code></a> typescript transformer.</p>\n' +
      '<p>For the transformer to work, it need to have a list of which components to transform (a list from the previous step) and\n' +
      'a list of which imports are safe (element imports, again a list from the previous step). Maybe the list of elements should be\n' +
      'created using file scanning? not sure regarding ordering of the bundling algorithm...</p>\n' +
      '<p>Given this transformer and the previous generation step, the bundler can create the main thread / window script.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>problem 3 - bundling</h2>\n' +
      '<p>Given the element with the <code>sandbox</code> imports, we generate the for the same element a sandbox root (<code>worker-root.ts</code>) using\n' +
      '<a href="../packages/compiler/lib/jay-file/jay-file-compiler.ts#962"><code>generateSandboxRootFile</code></a> which\n' +
      'is the start of the worker bundling process. Following the imports line, we import components as-is and element bridges generated\n' +
      'using <a href="../packages/compiler/lib/jay-file/jay-file-compiler.ts#916"><code>generateElementBridgeFile</code></a>.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>open question</h2>\n' +
      '<p>do we need to scan the files before the bundling process to generate whitelist of jay files / declerative files\n' +
      'that components are allowed to import? can we derive that during the bundling process itself?</p>\n' +
      '<h1>implementation</h1>\n' +
      '<p>The current idea is to implement a <code>jay start</code> command</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-shell"><span class="token function">jay</span> start</code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>The <code>jay start</code> command will start dev experience which will</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>scan source folder for <code>.jay.html</code> files and generate the relevant <code>.jay.html.d.ts</code> files, as well as the relevant\n' +
      'component refs files.</li>\n' +
      '<li>create bundles for the main window and the sandbox</li>\n' +
      '<li>auto generate a UI application that<ol>\n' +
      '<li>shows a list of components</li>\n' +
      '<li>for each component, a dedicated page with a board showing the component</li>\n' +
      '<li>preferably, a property panel to enter property values for the component</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<p>here we have an hidden challenge - how do we find all the components?</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>do we demand a configuration file that lists all the components that the UI app shows?</li>\n' +
      '<li>do we scan the folders for any Jay Component and showing it? what if the component is not independent and requires\n' +
      'some <code>context</code> or special <code>props</code> to operate?</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Log Methodology Note</h2>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Note:</strong> These design logs are written primarily for AI agents as part of the <a href="/design-log">Design Log methodology</a> and made accessible here for human readers. The language and structure are optimized for machine consumption — expect precise, specification-style prose rather than narrative documentation.</p>\n',
    description: '',
    date: '',
    tags: [],
    frontmatter: '{"title":"`Jay Start` - Compiling Sandbox Application","number":26}'
  }
}
