{
  post: {
    title: 'Reactive Pairing Fixes',
    content: '<h1>Reactive Paring fixes</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<p>Once implementing Reactive Pairing we started seeing anomalies in Jay that Reactives are flushed twice and when not needed.\n' +
      'This exposed a few problems and a design flaw with how Reactive Pairing works.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Reactive Pairing is the process at which when a reaction of reactive <code>A</code> is reading a signal from reactive <code>B</code>,\n' +
      'once reactive <code>B</code> is flushed it will also flush reactive <code>A</code> to ensure it reruns any reactions that depend on <code>B</code>&#39;s signal.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Reactive Pairing makes a lot of sense when <code>A</code> is a component reactive and <code>B</code> is a context reactive - at which point\n' +
      'we want all the component using the context signals to rerun reactions and potentially re-render.</p>\n' +
      '<p>The problem happens in two cases -</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><code>A</code> is a parent component, <code>B</code> a child component. during <code>A</code>&#39;s element render function (run as a reaction of <code>A</code>),\n' +
      'we also run the child component constructor. Any signal read from <code>B</code> not in a reaction of <code>B</code> (but in the constructor)\n' +
      'will trigger pairing such that when that <code>B</code> signal updates, <code>A</code> will flush.</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>A</code> is a parent component, <code>B</code> a child component. When <code>B</code> emits an event using an effect (which is a reaction of <code>B</code>)\n' +
      'when <code>A</code> handling the event reads any of <code>A</code> signal values it triggers pairing such that when <code>A</code> signal updates, <code>B</code> will flush.</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<p>The root cause is that Reactive Pairing is based on function call stack - when reading a signal of <code>B</code> within a reaction of <code>A</code>\n' +
      'we create pairing. <strong>This rule is way to generic</strong>, yet has big advantage for the component - context case - no need to\n' +
      'introduce a new API for the component to track context changes.</p>\n' +
      '<h1>Aside fixes as a result of the investigation</h1>\n' +
      '<p>Investigating this issue exposed a few other issues:</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Patch creating new objects when patching same values</h2>\n' +
      '<p>the <code>jay-json-patch</code> library patch function used to always create new objects, even if the patch did not update\n' +
      'any value. Because Jay is using immutable objects, it caused the system to think there is a change, while there is none.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Consider the object</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token keyword">const</span> obj <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token punctuation">{</span> a<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token number">1</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> b<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> c<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token number">3</span> <span class="token punctuation">}</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>Applying a patch such as</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token keyword">const</span> thePatch <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token punctuation">[</span>\n' +
      `  <span class="token punctuation">{</span> op<span class="token operator">:</span> REPLACE<span class="token punctuation">,</span> path<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token punctuation">[</span><span class="token string">'b'</span><span class="token punctuation">]</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> value<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token number">2</span> <span class="token punctuation">}</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span>\n` +
      `  <span class="token punctuation">{</span> op<span class="token operator">:</span> REPLACE<span class="token punctuation">,</span> path<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token punctuation">[</span><span class="token string">'c'</span><span class="token punctuation">]</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> value<span class="token operator">:</span> <span class="token number">3</span> <span class="token punctuation">}</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span>\n` +
      '<span class="token punctuation">]</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<p>does not change the object, and those patch now returns the original object</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-typescript"><span class="token function">expect</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token function">patch</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>obj<span class="token punctuation">,</span> thePatch<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">.</span><span class="token function">toBe</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>obj<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">;</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '<h2>createDerivedArray</h2>\n' +
      '<p>The createDerivedArray hook in <code>jay-component</code> used to be implemented using a Reactive for each mapped item.\n' +
      'This implementation was subject to the Reactive pairing issue above, as well as sub-optimal.</p>\n' +
      '<p>It is now implemented in a simpler and more efficient way.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>component mount</h2>\n' +
      '<p>Component mount function did not check if the component is mounted, and could have run twice in some cases.</p>\n' +
      '<p>We have changed component mount to be a signal with the actual mount / unmount logic a reaction, to ensure more consistent\n' +
      'behaviour</p>\n' +
      '<h1>Fixing Reactive Pairing</h1>\n' +
      '<p>It is clear that using only function call stack the reactive pairing is way too aggressive.</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>We want reactive pairing between context (providing signals) and components (reading signals).</li>\n' +
      '<li>We want reactive pairing between context (providing signals) and other contexts (reading signals).</li>\n' +
      '<li>We do not want components to provide signals for reactive pairing.</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h2>option 1</h2>\n' +
      '<p>We can extend Reactive with Reactive Pairing configuration such that</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Components will be configured as (reading signals)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Contexts will be configured as (reading or providing signals)</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<p>This option can still create unexpected pairings</p>\n' +
      '<h2>option 2</h2>\n' +
      '<p>Explicit pairing API, such that <code>A</code> will have to enable tracking <code>B</code>&#39;s state.\n' +
      'Can we create such an API that will not pollute the usage of context API? probably.</p>\n' +
      '<p>one way of doing so is to have an API <code>A.enableReadingfrom(B)</code> which means that <code>A</code> will now track signals of <code>B</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p>This is the preferred way, as it is not too eager, will likely not cause unexpected issues and also means\n' +
      '<code>A</code> points at <code>B</code>, and given <code>A</code> is a component and <code>B</code> a context, <code>A</code> is more likely to be cleared. Pointing <code>B</code> to <code>A</code>\n' +
      'can cause a memory leak, but the reverse is less likely to.</p>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Log Methodology Note</h2>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Note:</strong> These design logs are written primarily for AI agents as part of the <a href="/design-log">Design Log methodology</a> and made accessible here for human readers. The language and structure are optimized for machine consumption — expect precise, specification-style prose rather than narrative documentation.</p>\n',
    description: '',
    date: '',
    tags: [],
    frontmatter: '{"title":"Reactive Pairing Fixes","number":32}'
  }
}
