{
  post: {
    title: 'Composite Component',
    content: '<h1>Composite Component</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<p>When rendering a page that may have page component and a few applications installed, we need a way to compose the\n' +
      'different Jay components onto the page <code>jay-html</code>. In addition, if we have slowly rendered data, we need to compose\n' +
      'that data as well.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Consider a case when we have a page with product component, multilingual and A/B jay components. Each provides\n' +
      'a <code>makeJayStackComponent</code> definition, each can use contexts and each can have slowly and fast rendered data.\n' +
      'With Jay, unlike other frameworks, there is no &quot;box&quot; encapsulating each component, there is no separate <code>jsx</code> for each.\n' +
      'All are working with the same page design, or the same <code>jay-html</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p>On the server, we can run each application slowly and fast rendering independently and generate the partial <code>ViewState</code>\n' +
      'of each, then combine the <code>ViewState</code>s using <code>viewState = {...vs1, ...vs2, ...vs3}</code> and render the <code>jay-html</code>.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Partial rendering of the <code>jay-html</code> can give another benefit, saving CPU effort for rendering slowly rendered <code>ViewState</code>\n' +
      '(see <a href="36-partial-rendering">36 - Partial Rendering.md</a>), but at the same time this is an optimization we can\n' +
      'implement later.</p>\n' +
      '<p>On the client, we need to compose the client components. We can reuse the client <code>makeJayComponent</code> by introducing\n' +
      'a composite <code>JayComponent</code> that takes the client component of each jay stack component, the client contexts and the\n' +
      '<code>carryForward</code> data (from server), and takes the <code>ViewState</code> from the server as default, composing a single client Jay Component.</p>\n' +
      '<p>The Composite Client Jay Component will</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>take one or more Jay Stack interactive components, each with its own <code>Props &amp; CarryForward</code> and <code>Contexts</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>take default <code>ViewState</code> from server rendering</li>\n' +
      '<li>run each of the Jay Stack interactive components</li>\n' +
      '<li>compose the rendering result of each into the interactive <code>ViewState</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>compose the default <code>ViewState</code> with the interactive <code>ViewState</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>render the page</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<p>We note that we have not discussed client components, nor did we discuss component exported APIs.</p>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Log Methodology Note</h2>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Note:</strong> These design logs are written primarily for AI agents as part of the <a href="/design-log">Design Log methodology</a> and made accessible here for human readers. The language and structure are optimized for machine consumption — expect precise, specification-style prose rather than narrative documentation.</p>\n',
    description: '',
    date: '',
    tags: [],
    frontmatter: '{"title":"Composite Component","number":37}'
  }
}
