{
  post: {
    title: 'Jay-Stack Headless Component Resolution',
    content: '<h1>Jay Stack - Headless Component Resolution</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<h2>Context</h2>\n' +
      '<p>In Jay Stack, pages can be composed of &quot;headless&quot; components provided by installed applications (e.g., a &quot;Product List&quot; from a &quot;Store&quot; app). These components bring their own data contracts and behavior.</p>\n' +
      '<p>We support two ways to define which components are used on a page:</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Standard:</strong> Explicitly in the view template (<code>page.jay-html</code>) using <code>&lt;script type=&quot;application/jay-headless&quot;&gt;</code>.</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Headless/Configuration-driven:</strong> In a configuration file (<code>page.conf.yaml</code>) when the view template does not exist.</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h2>Problem</h2>\n' +
      '<p>When using <code>page.conf.yaml</code> (the headless approach), we define <em>what</em> we want to use, but we don&#39;t point directly to the file system paths of the contracts. The system needs a robust way to resolve the actual <code>.jay-contract</code> files to generate correct types and valid projects.</p>\n' +
      '<h2>Solution: Indirect Resolution via <code>app.conf.yaml</code></h2>\n' +
      '<p>The resolution uses a &quot;Foreign Key&quot; style relationship. The page configuration (or HTML) defines the <strong>Usage</strong>, while the installed application&#39;s configuration defines the <strong>Implementation Details</strong> (including paths).</p>\n' +
      '<h3>The Resolution Flow</h3>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Usage Definition (The Pointer)</strong>\n' +
      'Inside <code>src/pages/.../page.conf.yaml</code>, the developer defines the need for a component:</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-yaml"><span class="token keyword">used_components</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token punctuation">-</span> name<span class="token punctuation">:</span> product<span class="token punctuation">-</span>list <span class="token comment"># References the component name</span>\n' +
      '    src<span class="token punctuation">:</span> wix<span class="token punctuation">-</span>stores <span class="token comment"># References the installed app module</span>\n' +
      '    key<span class="token punctuation">:</span> products <span class="token comment"># The data key for the page contract</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Lookup (The Link)</strong>\n' +
      'The system uses the <code>src</code> value (<code>wix-stores</code>) to look up the application in the project&#39;s <code>installedApps</code> registry.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Definition (The Source of Truth)</strong>\n' +
      'The system reads the <code>app.conf.yaml</code> of the installed application (e.g., inside <code>node_modules/@wix/stores/</code> or <code>src/config/installedApps/wix-stores/</code>).</p>\n' +
      '<p>This file contains the mapping between the public component name and its internal file path:</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-yaml"><span class="token comment"># app.conf.yaml</span>\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">name</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span> wix<span class="token punctuation">-</span>stores\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">module</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span> @wix/stores\n' +
      '<span class="token keyword">pages</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span>\n' +
      '  <span class="token punctuation">-</span> name<span class="token punctuation">:</span> ProductPage\n' +
      '    headless_components<span class="token punctuation">:</span>\n' +
      '      <span class="token punctuation">-</span> name<span class="token punctuation">:</span> product<span class="token punctuation">-</span>list  <span class="token comment"># Match Found!</span>\n' +
      '        key<span class="token punctuation">:</span> products\n' +
      '        contract<span class="token punctuation">:</span> ./contracts/product<span class="token punctuation">-</span>list.jay<span class="token punctuation">-</span>contract  <span class="token comment"># &lt;--- The actual path</span></code></pre>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Path Resolution</strong>\n' +
      'The system resolves the relative path (<code>./contracts/product-list.jay-contract</code>) relative to the application&#39;s root.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h3>Why this design?</h3>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Decoupling:</strong> The page doesn&#39;t need to know the internal file structure of the installed app. It only needs to know the public name (<code>product-list</code>).</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Consistency:</strong> Both <code>page.conf.yaml</code> and <code>page.jay-html</code> use the exact same resolution mechanism.<ul>\n' +
      '<li>In <code>jay-html</code>: <code>&lt;script type=&quot;application/jay-headless&quot; src=&quot;wix-stores&quot; name=&quot;product-list&quot; ... /&gt;</code> relies on the same lookup in <code>app.conf.yaml</code> to find the contract.</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Refactoring Safety:</strong> The installed app can move its contract files around without breaking the consumer pages, as long as it updates its own <code>app.conf.yaml</code>.</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<h3>Diagram</h3>\n' +
      '<div class="md-mermaid"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 293.258 722.8" width="293.258" height="722.8">\n' +
      '<style>\n' +
