{
  post: {
    title: 'Gemini Agent Plugin For Jay-Stack',
    content: '<h1>Gemini Agent Plugin for Jay-Stack</h1>\n' +
      '<blockquote>\n' +
      '<p><em>Written for AI agents. See <a href="#log-methodology-note">Log Methodology Note</a> below for details.</em></p>\n' +
      '</blockquote>\n' +
      '<h2>Background</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>What exists today</h3>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>WebMCP plugin</strong> (Design Log #91) — automatically exposes page interactions as WebMCP tools to browsers with <code>navigator.modelContext</code>. Client-only plugin. Relies on an external AI agent (browser-side) to call the tools.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>AutomationAPI</strong> (<code>@jay-framework/runtime-automation</code>) — wraps Jay components:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li><code>getPageState()</code> → <code>{ viewState, interactions, customEvents }</code></li>\n' +
      '<li><code>triggerEvent(eventType, coordinate)</code> → trigger UI events</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>getInteraction(coordinate)</code> → find specific interaction (returns DOM element)</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>onStateChange(callback)</code> → subscribe to changes</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Server Actions</strong> — <code>makeJayAction</code>/<code>makeJayQuery</code> exported from plugins, discovered at startup, served at <code>/_jay/actions/:actionName</code>. Client calls via <code>createActionCaller(name, method)</code> → HTTP fetch.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Action Registry</strong> (<code>ActionRegistry</code>) — server-side registry. <code>actionRegistry.getNames()</code> lists all registered actions. <code>actionRegistry.execute(name, input)</code> runs them.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>Plugin config pattern</strong> — plugins read config from <code>config/&lt;filename&gt;.yaml</code> (see <code>wix-server-client</code>). Setup handler creates template, validates credentials.</p>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '<li><p><strong>WebMCP tools architecture</strong> — the webmcp plugin builds <code>ToolDescriptor[]</code> from <code>AutomationAPI</code>:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Generic tools: <code>get-page-state</code>, <code>list-interactions</code>, <code>trigger-interaction</code>, <code>fill-input</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>Semantic tools: <code>click-{refName}</code>, <code>fill-{refName}</code>, <code>toggle-{refName}</code> auto-generated per interaction</li>\n' +
      '<li>Same <code>ToolDescriptor</code> type: <code>{ name, description, inputSchema, execute }</code></li>\n' +
      '<li>Tools only (no resources or prompts) — aligned with Chrome Canary&#39;s <code>modelContext</code> API</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Problem</h2>\n' +
      '<p>The WebMCP plugin requires a <strong>WebMCP-enabled browser</strong> (Chrome Canary). Most users don&#39;t have that. We want to bring an AI agent <strong>directly into any jay-stack page</strong> — a chat interface where users can interact with a Gemini-powered agent that can:</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Read</strong> current page state</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Drive page interactions</strong> (click buttons, fill inputs, select options) — same capabilities as webmcp</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Call server actions</strong> exposed by any plugin</li>\n' +
      '<li><strong>Converse</strong> with the user via a chat UI</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<p>The plugin should:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Use the <strong>Gemini API</strong> (server-side, with function calling)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Expose a <strong>contract</strong> for building a chat UI in jay-html</li>\n' +
      '<li>Reuse the same tool infrastructure as webmcp (same <code>ToolDescriptor</code> type and generation logic)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Accept config (API key) via the standard plugin config pattern</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<hr>\n' +
      '<h2>Questions and Answers</h2>\n' +
      '<h3>Q1: Where does the LLM call happen — client or server?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Server. The Gemini API key must stay server-side. The flow:</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>User types message in chat UI (client)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Client sends message to server action</li>\n' +
      '<li>Server calls Gemini API with message + tool definitions</li>\n' +
      '<li>Gemini may request tool calls → server executes them (page automation tools run on client, server actions run on server)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Results returned to Gemini → may generate more tool calls or a text response</li>\n' +
      '<li>Final text response sent back to client → displayed in chat</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<h3>Q2: How do page automation tools (which need DOM access) run from the server?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> They can&#39;t run on the server directly. The flow is:</p>\n' +
      '<ol>\n' +
      '<li>Server sends a <strong>tool call request</strong> to the client (via the action response)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Client executes the tool against <code>AutomationAPI</code> (same code as webmcp)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Client sends tool results back to server (via another action call)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Server continues the Gemini conversation with the tool results</li>\n' +
      '</ol>\n' +
      '<p>This means a single user message may require <strong>multiple round-trips</strong>:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Client → Server (user message) → Gemini → tool calls needed</li>\n' +
      '<li>Server → Client (pending tool calls) → Client executes → Client → Server (tool results)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Server → Gemini (tool results) → final response or more tool calls</li>\n' +
      '<li>Eventually: Server → Client (final text response)</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<h3>Q3: Should we use streaming or request/response?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Start with request/response. The multi-round-trip nature (Q2) already provides incremental feedback. Streaming adds complexity (SSE/WebSocket) that we can add later.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Q4: How does the plugin discover available tools?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Two categories:</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Page automation tools</strong> (client-side): Generated from <code>AutomationAPI</code> exactly like the webmcp plugin — reuse <code>buildSemanticTools()</code> and the generic tool builders from <code>@jay-framework/webmcp-plugin</code>. Serialized as tool definitions (name, description, inputSchema) and sent to the server.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Server actions</strong> (server-side): Discovered from the <code>ActionRegistry</code> at runtime. <code>actionRegistry.getNames()</code> + metadata for each action. These run server-side and don&#39;t need client round-trips.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Q5: How is the Gemini tool schema derived from our ToolDescriptor?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Direct mapping. Our <code>ToolInputSchema</code> is already JSON Schema (<code>type: &#39;object&#39;, properties, required</code>) — Gemini&#39;s function calling accepts this format. The mapping:</p>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="">ToolDescriptor.name → FunctionDeclaration.name\n' +
      'ToolDescriptor.description → FunctionDeclaration.description\n' +
      'ToolDescriptor.inputSchema → FunctionDeclaration.parameters</code></pre>\n' +
      '<h3>Q6: What does the chat contract look like?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The contract exposes tags for building a chat UI with two display modes:</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Full mode:</strong> Shows all messages in a scrollable list (classic chat UI).</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Compact mode:</strong> Shows only the input + the last question/answer pair. An expand button reveals full history. This is useful for embedding the agent as a small widget on a page without dominating the layout.</p>\n' +
      '<p>Tags:</p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Messages list (repeated sub-contract): role, content, timestamp</li>\n' +
      '<li>Text input for user message</li>\n' +
      '<li>Send button</li>\n' +
      '<li>Loading state variant</li>\n' +
      '<li>Error state</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>isExpanded</code> variant (boolean) — controls full vs compact view</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>toggleExpand</code> interactive button — switches between modes</li>\n' +
      '<li><code>lastUserMessage</code> / <code>lastAssistantMessage</code> data — shortcut for compact mode (avoids iterating the full list)</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<h3>Q7: How does the plugin manage conversation state?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Client-side. The server is <strong>stateless</strong> — no session map, no memory cleanup, no scaling concerns.</p>\n' +
      '<p>The client component holds the full <code>ConversationState</code> (message history in the Gemini format). On each request, the client sends the entire conversation history to the server action. The server passes it to Gemini, gets the response, and returns the updated history back to the client.</p>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Why client-side:</strong></p>\n' +
      '<ul>\n' +
      '<li>Server is stateless — no session management, no memory leaks, no cleanup timers</li>\n' +
      '<li>Scales trivially (any server instance can handle any request)</li>\n' +
      '<li>Page refresh / navigation loses state naturally (expected for a chat widget)</li>\n' +
      '<li>The API key is still server-side — only conversation messages travel over the wire</li>\n' +
      '</ul>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> Larger payloads (full history on every request). Acceptable for a chat widget — typical conversations are tens of messages, not thousands. If conversations get very long, we can truncate older messages or summarize.</p>\n' +
      '<h3>Q8: Should action metadata (input schema, description) be available for the LLM?</h3>\n' +
      '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. Currently <code>RegisteredAction</code> has <code>actionName</code>, <code>method</code>, <code>handler</code>, but no <code>inputSchema</code> or <code>description</code>. Design Log #84 Q5b designed an <code>.action.yaml</code> format but it was never implemented.</p>\n' +
      '<p>We introduce <strong><code>.jay-action</code> files</strong> — one file per action, mirroring the one-file-per-contract pattern (<code>.jay-contract</code>). Each file describes an action&#39;s input schema, output schema, and purpose in a format that maps directly to Gemini function declarations.</p>\n' +
      '<h4><code>.jay-action</code> file format</h4>\n' +
      '<pre class="md-code"><code class="language-yaml"><span class="token comment"># search-products.jay-action</span>\n' +
